===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈtɪt fə(ɹ) ˈtæt/}}
*{{a|GA|CA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈtɪt fɚ ˈtæt/|/ˈtɪt fɝ ˈtæt/}}
* {{audio|En-au-tit for tat.ogg|Audio (AU)|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
Origin uncertain; conjectured to be from {{der|en|nl|[[dit]] [[voor]] [[dat]]||this for that}}, {{der|en|fr|[[tant]] [[pour]] [[tant]]||equal for equal}},<ref>{{cite-book|entry=tit|editors=E[lizabeth] M[cLaren] Kirkpatrick and C[atherine] M. Schwarz|title=The Wordsworth Dictionary of Idioms|series=Wordsworth Reference Series|location=Ware, Hertfordshire|publisher={{w|Wordsworth Reference}}|year=1993|page=391|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=8C7k7ZW3dIEC&pg=PA391|isbn=978-1-85326-309-5}}.</ref> or {{cog|en|tip for tap||a blow for a blow}}. The vowel sequence follows a typical [[w:apophony#Ablaut-motivated compounding|ablaut reduplication]] pattern.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} [[equivalent#Adjective|Equivalent]] [[retribution]]; an act of [[return#Verb|returning]] [[exactly]] what one gets; an [[eye for an eye]].
#: {{ux|en|If you hit me, I’ll hit you back; '''tit for tat'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|author=[Edward Ward]|authorlink=Ned Ward|title=The Dutch Riddle: Or, A Character of a Hairy Monster, often Found in Holland, &c.|location=[London?]|publisher=Sold by the booksellers of London and Westminster|year=c. 1706|page=2|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=hbZaAAAAcAAJ&pg=PP8|oclc=66791273|passage=Could it but for a longer Space / Lengthen the Bliſs it let's us taſte, / Who would not [[doat]] on't? But alas! / The Joy's too exquiſite to laſt. / Two white ''Herculean'' Pillars prop / The tufted Gin, the tempting Snare; / When they divide, then in we pop, / Before we well know where we are. / Then that for this, and '''tit for tat'''; / But when the pleaſing Minute's flown, / As uſeleſs it returns the Bait, / And both look fooliſh when 'tis done.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|author=[Henry] Carey|authorlink=Henry Carey (writer)|title=The Honest Yorkshire-man. A Ballad Farce. As It is Acted at the Theatre-Royal|location=Dublin|publisher=Printed and sold by Thomas Wilkinson, in Winetavern Street, at the corner of Cook-Street; [...]|year=c. 1775|page=23|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=-xdgAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA23|oclc=723131215|passage=This is but '''Tit for Tat''', young Gentleman. Your Father wanted to get my Eſtate from me; and I have got the Wife he intended for you. All's fair, Sir.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|author=Philip Wharton|chapter=II|title=Heart or Head. [...] In Two Volumes|location=London|publisher=Charles J. Skeet, 10, [[w:King William Street, London|King William Street]], {{w|Charing Cross}}|year=1864|volume=II|page=59|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/heartorheadbyph01thomgoog#page/n65/mode/1up|oclc=12121615|passage="You'll never get her over like that. You must coax her." / "Coax the devil. Do you think I'm going to be beat by a horse?" / "It would only be '''tit for tat'''; you beat her, so she has a right to beat you."}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|author=Georges Feydeau|authorlink=Georges Feydeau|author2=Norman R. Shapiro, transl.|chapter=Hortense Said, ‘No Skin Off My Ass!’|editor=Maurice Charney|title=Comedy: New Perspectives|series=New York Literary Forum|seriesvolume=1|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=New York Literary Forum|month=spring|year=1978|page=307|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=51C0AAAAIAAJ&pg=PA307|isbn=978-0-931196-00-3|issn=0149-1040|passage=YVETTE. I have no idea what to make Monsieur for dinner. / FOLLBRAGUET. ''(beside himself)'' Well, that's no skin off my ass, damn it! / YVETTE. ''(snippily, giving him '''tit for tat''')'' Well, it's certainly no skin off mine, Monsieur!}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|author=William M. LeoGrande|authorlink=William M. LeoGrande|author2=Peter Kornbluh|authorlink2=Peter Kornbluh|chapter=[[w:Bill Clinton|Clinton]]: From Calibrated Response to Parallel Positive Steps|title=Back Channel to Cuba: The Hidden History of Negotiations between Washington and Havana|location=Chapel Hill, N.C.|publisher={{w|University of North Carolina Press}}|year=2014|page=279|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=zOZjBAAAQBAJ&pg=PA279|isbn=978-1-4696-1763-3|passage=As part of the planning for a new initiative on Cuba, the NSC &#91;[[w:United States National Security Council|National Security Council]]&#93; also revived the concept of "calibrated response." A new staffer was tasked to develop a list of "'''tit-for-tats'''" with Cuba—which sanctions might be lifted or softened in response to clear steps toward democracy by the [[w:Fidel Castro|[Fidel] Castro]] regime.}}
#* {{quote-web|lang=en|author=Arjun Kharpal|title=China Warns of ‘Tit for Tat’ on iPhone Sales if Trump Starts Trade War|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/tech-news/china-warns-tit-tat-iphone-sales-if-trump-starts-trade-n683446|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170908001249/https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/tech-news/china-warns-tit-tat-iphone-sales-if-trump-starts-trade-n683446|archivedate=8 September 2017|section=title|work={{w|NBC News}}|date=14 November 2016|passage=China Warns of ‘'''Tit for Tat'''’ on iPhone Sales if Trump Starts Trade War}}

