===Etymology 2===
[[File:Bowler.hats.portobello.london.arp.jpg|thumb|[[bowler hat|Bowler hats]]&nbsp;– a type of “tit for tat”&nbsp;– on sale at the [[w:Portobello Road|Portobello Road Market]] in [[London]], [[England]], UK]]

''Tit for tat'' is a [[rhyme#Noun|rhyme]] for {{m|en|hat}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|hat}}{{lb|en|Cockney rhyming slang}} A [[hat]]. {{defdate|from late 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|author=A. R. Homer|title=The Sobs of Autumn’s Violins: A Novel of War and Love|location=Coral Springs, Fla.|publisher={{w|Llumina Press}}|year=2005|page=148|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=p1BGERvoN_IC&pg=PA148|isbn=978-1-59526-482-4|passage=Fred came from Cheapside in London&nbsp;– an official Cockney, born within earshot of the bells of St. Mary-le-Bow&nbsp;– and he taught her Cockney rhyming slang. She loved its lighthearted logic: the last word in a common phrase rhymed with the word it was to replace. So "'''tit for tat'''" was "hat", and "trouble and strife" was "wife". But the best part was that you usually dropped the rhyming words and used only the first words, so "your wife's hat" became "your trouble's titfer".|brackets=on}}
#* {{quote-book|lang=en|author=Geoff Tibballs|chapter=Tit for tat|title=The Ultimate Cockney Geezer’s Guide to Rhyming Slang|location=London|publisher=Ebury Press, {{w|Ebury Publishing}}|year=2008|page=182|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=w_ohWEKQsqMC&pg=PA182|isbn=978-0-09-192748-6|passage='''Tit for tat''' hat / The phrase ‘'''tit for tat'''’ {{...}} emerged as a rhyme for ‘hat’ in the late nineteenth century and was subsequently condensed to ‘titfer’ around 1930, in which form it enjoyed unparalleled success at a time when virtually everyone wore a hat.|brackets=on}}

